    <node><name>1</name><description>Inverted</description><answertest>NumAbsolute</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>kinv</tans><testoptions>0.001</testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-2-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>That makes \(\overrightarrow{AB} = k\,\overrightarrow{AC}\): the division is the wrong way round. Check with the y-components: \(k \times ({@d[2]@})\) must equal \({@c2-A[2]@}\), but your \(k\) gives \({@chk@}\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-2-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Use the y-components, which are known in both vectors: \(k \times ({@d[2]@})\) must equal \({@c2-A[2]@}\). Your \(k\) gives \({@chk@}\).</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
